---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ForestPlot from '../../components/ForestPlot.astro';
import EffectDots from '../../components/EffectDots.astro';
import RankDots from '../../components/RankDots.astro';
import DataExplorer from '../../components/DataExplorer.astro';
import Placeholder from '../../components/Placeholder.astro';
import data from '../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/trust-by-domain.json';
import effects from '../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/effects-by-study.json';
import ranked from '../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/trust-by-recommender.json';
import study1 from '../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/study1-tests.json';
import meta from '../../data/trust-in-medical-ai/meta.json';
import { href } from '../../utils/url';
---

<Base
  title="Trust in medical AI"
  description="Six preregistered studies on why people trust a doctor's advice more than an AI's, from my Georgetown dissertation."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="back small"><a href={href('/projects/')}>All projects</a></p>
      <h1>Trust in medical AI</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        My dissertation at Georgetown University, completed in 2024. Six preregistered studies, with more than 3,000
        participants recruited, on why people trust a doctor's advice more than an AI's.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <section class="section">
        <h2>The question</h2>
        <p>
          Medical AI has shown diagnostic and prescriptive ability that rivals doctors. Yet when a doctor and an AI give
          people the same recommendation, people trust the doctor more. I wanted to know what drives that gap, and
          whether it can be closed.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="section chart" aria-labelledby="gap-title">
      <h2 id="gap-title">The gap holds across medical domains</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Study 2 asked 106 adults how much they trusted a doctor and an AI making the same medical judgment. Hover over
        a row or tab to it for the exact statistics.
      </p>
      <ForestPlot
        rows={data.rows}
        xMin={-1}
        xMax={5}
        xLabel="Doctor minus AI, in trust rating points"
        leftLabel="AI trusted more"
        rightLabel="Doctor trusted more"
      />
      <p class="small muted source">
        Source: {meta.source} Study 2, Table 7.
        <a href={meta.publicLinks.study2Preregistration}>Preregistration</a> and
        <a href={meta.publicLinks.study2DataAndAnalysis}>data and analysis</a> are on OSF.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section chart" aria-labelledby="rank-title">
      <h2 id="rank-title">Who people trust, from doctors down to chatbots</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        In Study 1, {ranked.rows[0].n} people rated how much they would trust each of eleven recommenders for medical decisions.
        Doctors and nurses sit at the top. Every AI sits below the human experts, near the level people gave a layperson. A doctor
        who uses AI lands in between.
      </p>
      <RankDots rows={ranked.rows} scale={ranked.scale} xLabel="Trust, from 0 (not at all) to 10 (completely)" />
      <p class="small muted source">Source: {ranked.source} Hover over a row or tab to it for the details.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section chart" aria-labelledby="human-title">
      <h2 id="human-title">Making the AI humanlike helped only when people compared</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        I described one AI in a more humanlike way, with a name and human verbs (plus a voice from Study 4 on), and gave the same advice in
        every condition. Each row is a study. When each person saw one recommender, the humanlike AI did not gain
        significantly. When the same people judged all three side by side, it did.
      </p>
      <EffectDots rows={effects.rows} xMax={1} xLabel="Cohen's d versus the standard AI" />
      <p class="small muted source">
        Source: {effects.source} Hover over a row or tab to it for the test statistics.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <section class="section">
        <h2>What I found</h2>
        <ul class="findings">
          <li>
            <strong>People trusted the doctor more than a standard AI in every one of the first five studies.</strong>
            That held across medical domains, decision types and self-relevant scenarios.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>People saw the doctor as more capable.</strong> They rated doctors higher on both intuition
            (d = 0.58) and quantitative reasoning (d = 0.56), and trusted them more to make medical decisions
            (d = 0.79). These are Study 1 results.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A humanlike AI helped in one design but not another.</strong> When people judged both AIs, they
            trusted the humanlike one more (Study 5, d = 0.46). When each person saw only one AI, the difference was not
            significant (Study 3, d = 0.07).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Trust did not change behavior.</strong> In a 15-day health intervention (560 recruited, 455
            retained), whether a doctor or an AI made the recommendation did not change adherence. I report it as a
            null result.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>How I measured it</h2>
        <p>
          I collected trust ratings and a behavioral measure called weight on advice, which records how far people move
          their own judgment toward a recommendation. Every study was preregistered.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>What this means for product teams</h2>
        <p>
          If a health AI is trusted less than a clinician, the gap persisted across every medical domain I tested, so
          adjusting the content alone may not close it. Test how people perceive the decision-maker, and measure what
          they do, not only what they say they trust.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="section chart" id="study1-data" aria-labelledby="data-title">
      <h2 id="data-title">Explore the Study 1 data</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        The cleaned data and the analysis code are public. Pick any two recommenders and a rating to see how the 188
        people answered. Nothing here identifies a participant: I dropped IP addresses, locations, timestamps, IDs and
        all demographic questions, and shuffled the participant numbers.
      </p>
      <DataExplorer rec={ranked.rows} />

      <h3 class="tests-title">The preregistered tests</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="tests">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Hypothesis</th>
              <th scope="col">First minus second</th>
              <th scope="col">Means</th>
              <th scope="col"><abbr title="Paired t test: compares two ratings given by the same people. Larger values mean a bigger difference relative to the noise.">t</abbr></th>
              <th scope="col"><abbr title="Probability of a difference this large if there were no real difference. Below .05 is called significant.">p</abbr></th>
              <th scope="col"><abbr title="Cohen's d for paired ratings: mean difference over the SD of the differences. About 0.2 is small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large.">d</abbr></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              study1.tests.map((t) => (
                <tr class={t.supported ? 'yes' : 'no'}>
                  <th scope="row">
                    <span class="hid">{t.id}</span> {t.text}
                  </th>
                  <td>{t.a} minus {t.b}</td>
                  <td>{t.meanA.toFixed(1)} v {t.meanB.toFixed(1)}</td>
                  <td>{t.t.toFixed(2)}</td>
                  <td>{t.p < 0.001 ? '< .001' : t.p.toFixed(3).replace(/^0/, '')}</td>
                  <td>{t.d.toFixed(2)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted source">
        Paired t tests on the released data, N = 185 to 188 depending on missing ratings. Decision items are on a 0 to 100
        scale, recommender ratings on 0 to 10. Hypothesis 1.b.2 was marginal (p = .061) and 1.a.1 showed no difference.
        Hypothesis 2.a.2 was not preregistered. How the file was cleaned is in <a href="https://github.com/mrleitao/Psycho_Robot#study-1-data-release">the project README</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section slots" aria-labelledby="more-title">
      <h2 id="more-title">More charts for this page</h2>
      <div class="slot-grid">
        <Placeholder
          title="Condition means for Studies 3 to 5"
          provide={[
            'Mean trust and 95% confidence interval for each condition in each study, if you want means instead of effect sizes',
            'The figure from the dissertation, if you want the chart to match it',
          ]}
        />
        <Placeholder
          title="Adherence over 15 days in Study 6"
          provide={[
            'Daily adherence by condition, as a summary table or a de-identified file',
            'Confirmation that the consent form allows public release',
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .tests-title { margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  .tests { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: var(--display); font-size: 0.92rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .tests th, .tests td { padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
  .tests thead th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 500; }
  .tests tbody th { font-weight: 400; min-width: 15rem; }
  .tests .hid { font-weight: 600; margin-right: 0.35rem; }
  .tests td:nth-child(5) { white-space: nowrap; }
  .tests tr.no td { color: var(--muted); }
  .tests abbr { text-decoration: underline dotted; text-underline-offset: 3px; cursor: help; }
  .back {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .chart {
    max-width: 680px;
  }

  .sub {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.75rem;
  }

  .source {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .findings {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  .findings li {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .slot-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .slot-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
